 ],
 "literal": "Young men will not wear, women with bangles will not pluck / gently pulling down the arm of the good lute / the male bard will not wear, the female bard will not adorn / so that manliness appeared, overcoming other men / after Sathan of the strong spear died / do you still bloom, oh jasmine, in the land of Ollaiyur?",
 "translation": "Young men will no longer wear you; women with bangles will no longer pluck you. Gently bending the arm of the fine lute, the bard will not wear you, nor will the songstress adorn herself. Now that Sathan of the mighty spear, who conquered other warriors to make his valor shine, has died, how can you still bloom, oh jasmine, in this land of Ollaiyur?",
 "modern": "இளைஞர்கள் உன்னைச் சூடமாட்டார்கள்; வளையல் அணிந்த பெண்கள் உன்னைக் கொய்யமாட்டார்கள். நல்ல யாழின் தண்டை மெல்ல வளைத்து, பாணன் உன்னைச் சூடமாட்டான்; பாடினியும் உன்னை அணியமாட்டாள். தன் ஆண்மை வெளிப்படுமாறு பகை வீரர்களை வென்ற வலிமையான வேலைக் கொண்ட சாத்தன் இறந்த பிறகு, ஒல்லையூர் நாட்டில் முல்லைப் பூவே! நீ இன்னும் மலர்கின்றாயோ?",
 "explanation": "This poem is set in the Puram genre under the theme of 'Kaiyaru Nilai' (grief over death), composed by Kudavayil Keerathanar on the death of Peruñcāttaṉ, the chief of Ollaiyūr. The poet addresses the wild jasmine (mullai) flower blooming in the land of Ollaiyur after the death of its heroic leader. He laments that since the great warrior Sathan, who proved his valor in battle, has passed away, there is no one left to appreciate or wear the jasmine. Young men, women, bards, and songstresses have all abandoned wearing flowers in their deep mourning. The poet asks the jasmine flower with poignant grief how it can still heartlessly bloom when the entire land is in sorrow.",
